SECTION 1: Introduction and Project Goal
1.1 What Problem Does This App Solve?
Computers and phones become full of lots of images, PDFs of homework, audio files and random downloads in various folders. Sometimes you have several versions of the same file, saved with different names (e.g. DSC_005.jpg and DSC_005_copy.jpg). This causes wastage of gigabytes of storage.
1.2 Why I Built It This Way
Project Aim: A digital photo and file management application that scans through a folder and sorts them into six categories. It can find 100% duplicate files and provides charts for how much space is being used. It will sort them very neatly into folders.
Safety: Designed to ensure you never accidentally lose any data. This is done by using Dry-Run Previews, Undo and Renaming of filename collisions.
SECTION 2: How the System Works (Overall Flowchart)
2.1 Step-by-Step Program Flow
The app can be broken down into 4 simple steps:

1) Choose folder: The user chooses the folder they want to scan in main.py and stores it in the variable folder_path.

2) Scan all files: In scanner.py, the folder_path is opened and all subfolders are scanned. All the file names, sizes, dates and categories are stored in a list called files.

3) Add camera information & Find duplicates: In image_analyzer.py, the photos have additional information added about the camera. In duplicate_finder.py, all the file sizes and SHA256 fingerprints are compared. Identical twins are then grouped together in a list called duplicate_groups.

4) Display, organise or export: main.py displays all the results on the screen. The user can then use the organizer.py to sort the files into nice folders or use exporter.py to save a report.


