	SECTION 3: Breakdown of Main Code Files and Variables
3.1 main.py (The App Window and Dashboard)
The main.py file is responsible for providing the application with a brain and GUI. The main.py file uses the tkinter module in Python to create buttons, cards and 4 main tabs (Overview, All Files, Duplicates and Organise). This is done by using the FileOrganizerApp class.

Linking All Files: Once a folder has been selected by the user, main.py will call on the scanning, duplicate finding and image analysis functions. The data from all 3 will be stored in a single dictionary (self.scan_data).

Smooth UI: As scanning thousands of files can take some time, main.py will run this in the background using threading.Thread. This ensures the application window and progress bar run smoothly.


3.2 scanner.py (Finding and Grouping Every File)
The scanner.py file is used to explore the selected directory. It will search through all subdirectories and get information on files.


Scan Directory: The scan_directory function uses os.walk to automatically scan through the main directory and subdirectories. This is done without scanning any hidden system files.


Classify Files Into 6 Categories: The classify_file function uses the CATEGORY_MAP dictionary to check the file extension (.jpg, .pdf, .mp3 etc.) and assign the category as being Images, Documents, Audio, Video, Archives or Other.

Summary: All file names, paths, sizes (in bytes) and dates are stored in a list called files. The total number of files and sizes are calculated for each category in the category_summary.


3.3 duplicate_finder.py (Two-Step Twin Detection Using Size and SHA256)
The duplicate_finder.py file acts as the detective of the application. It identifies any perfect duplicates of files that may have different names or be stored in different subfolders.

The find_duplicates function will look at each file and group them together based on their size_bytes. This will create groups of files with the same exact byte size. Files that are not the same size will be skipped to save time.

The compute_sha256 function will then look at each file in the same size group and generate a unique digital fingerprint for each file. This will look at each file in 64KB chunks.

If there are two or more files with the same fingerprint they will be stored in the duplicate_groups list. The first file to be kept will be marked and then the wasted_bytes will add up the sizes of the extra files so the user knows how much storage space they can save.


3.4 image_analyzer.py (Reading Photo Details and Camera Info)
The image_analyzer.py is used to analyse the images. When an image file is found by the scanner the image will be opened using the Pillow (PIL) library in Python to extract extra information.
Extracting Image Information: The extract_image_metadata function extracts the width, height, RGB colour and format (JPEG or PNG) of the image. It then combines the width and height to create a resolution such as 1920x1080.


Extracting Camera Information (EXIF): Many images contain extra digital information called EXIF. This is used to extract the camera brand (camera_make), camera model (camera_model) and date the image was taken (date_taken).


Fallback: If there is no camera information on the downloaded image or the Pillow library is not installed on the computer the function will return N/A to prevent the app crashing.


3.5 organizer.py (Dry Run Preview, Safe Moving, and Undo Feature)
This file acts as the 'Safe Mover' of the project. It organises all the messy files into neat folders based on category, date and size. No files are lost or overwritten.
The first step in this process is to create a dry run using the preview_organization function. This will show a dry run version of the plan to move the files. This will show the original file path and destination file path.


Next once the user has clicked execute the files will be moved into the new folders using the execute_organization function. Whilst doing this the _resolve_collision function will add a suffix to the file name such as '_1' or '_2' if there is a file with the same name.


Finally all the moves are added to a memory list called _undo_stack. When the user clicks undo_last_operation it will read the list backwards and return all the files to their original location.


3.6 exporter.py (Saving Reports to CSV and JSON)
This file acts as a "Report Printer" which will allow the user to export all the scan results to their computer. They can then open it up in Excel, Google Sheets or another program.


1) Export the full file list  This function will take all the scanned files and use the python CSV tool to put the file name, category, size, date and photo information into a neat spreadsheet.


2) Export the duplicates report  This function will create a special spreadsheet containing only the duplicated files, their shared SHA256 hash and how much space they are wasting.


3) Export the full JSON backup  This function will use the python JSON tool to create a neat backup file containing the summary statistics, category counts, duplicated groups and file information.


