SECTION 4: Key Programming Concepts I Used
4.1 Why Check File Size Before Hashing? (Speed Optimization)
The application compares the SHA256 hash, a digital fingerprint created by hashing the entire file. Reading every piece of data from the file is however extremely slow for large photos and videos.


The application duplicate_finder.py first filters the files based on their size:
1) Size Filter - The application loops through each file and adds them to a dictionary called size_groups based on their size_bytes. If the file has a unique size then there cannot be any twins so it can skip the file.


2) Hash - Only run compute_sha256 on the groups with 2 or more files with the same size.
Checking the file size is quick as the application can do this without opening the file. This means that around 90% of files can be skipped instantly. Meaning duplicates can be found almost instantly.


4.2 Reading Files in 64 KB Chunks (Saving RAM Memory)
When generating the SHA256 fingerprint, the app will need to process the data in the file. If the app attempted to open a large 4GB video file it would cause the RAM (temporary memory) of the computer to be filled, and cause the program to crash.

To prevent this from happening the duplicate_finder.py script reads the files in small chunks using CHUNK_SIZE = 65536:


Small Bites: By using the while loop, the app only ever loads 64KB of the file into memory at once, feeds it into the hasher and then clears that from memory. It then moves onto the next part of the file.
Can Handle Any File Size: This is similar to drinking a large bottle of water in sips. This means that the app can handle very large movies or zip files with very little RAM usage.




4.3 Preventing File Overwrites with Numbered Names
When moving files from multiple folders into one folder (such as Images) there may be cases where there are 2 completely different images with the same name (e.g. IMG_001.jpg). If the app were to move this file normally then it would overwrite the original image.

In order to make sure no files are accidentally lost, the app uses a helper function within the code called _resolve_collision.


File Splitting: First the file name is split into 2 separate parts. A stem name (IMG_001) and the file type (.jpg).


Renaming: If the file name already exists within the destination folder, a while loop will add a number onto the end of the file name to rename the file. This will rename the file to IMG_001_1.jpg (or _2, _3 if needed) until there is an entirely free spot.
This ensures that both files are stored safely next to each other and no user data is accidentally deleted.


4.4 How the Undo Stack Works
If the user accidentally sorts their folder, then there is an inbuilt 'Undo' button in the app. This is handled in the organizer.py file using a list called '_undo_stack'.


A stack in programming works the same way as a stack of dinner plates. The last dish to go on top will be the first to come off:


Save Actions: Every time the app moves a group of files, a list of old_path and new_path tuples will be added to the top of the '_undo_stack'.


Undo: When the user clicks on Undo, the undo_last_operation function will remove the most recent group from the top of the '_undo_stack'. It will then iterate backwards through the list using reversed() and move each file back into its original folder.


All empty folders are also cleared up so the user's computer looks like nothing has changed.


